The Tersenet manual
===================

These are the sources of the Tersenet manual: documentation of the whole
system written as Tersenet articles (ARTICLE.md), read in the reader (M
on its timeline opens the index) and published with `terse manual
publish`.

- `manifest` lists the articles in reading order, the index first; each is
  `<name>.txt`, whose first line is `# <title>` (the reader shows it as the
  article's title, and `terse manual publish` uses it as the entry's).
- A link to another article of the manual is written `terse:{<name>}`,
  usually as a link with text: `[the page device](terse:{device-page})`.
- An article's ID is the hash of its text, which holds the IDs of the
  articles it links to, so the links must form no cycle. The rule that
  keeps them so: an article links only to articles that come after it in
  `manifest`. Mention an earlier article by its title, without a link.
- `terse manual build` (run from the repository's root; `--dir` names
  another directory) resolves the links, in dependency order, and writes
  `build/<name>.txt` and `build/manifest` (`<blob ID> <name> <title>` per
  line). It fails on a link to an unknown name or a cycle. The build is
  checked in: `terse-net` embeds it (every host puts the articles in its
  store at start), and programs/reader/build.sh and programs/shell/build.sh compile the index's ID
  into the reader and the shell, so after a change run, in order:

      cargo run -p terse-cli --bin terse -- manual build
      programs/reader/build.sh
      programs/shell/build.sh
      cargo run -p terse-cli --bin terse -- manual site --out site

  The last one regenerates `site/`, the manual as a static website
  (checked in; see "The website" in the top-level README).

  `cargo test -p terse-cli --test manual` checks that the build and
  `site/` are up to date, that every link resolves, and that the manual names every call,
  event, content operation and status of the machine specification (SPEC.md and spec/;
  `--test spec` checks their ids, citations and tables); `--test reader` that
  the reader holds the current index's ID and shows every article whole.
- Keep each article under about 14 KB (built, with its links resolved, it
  must stay within the 16,384 bytes a host downloads automatically, which
  `--test reader` checks, so it opens at once), preformatted
  and fenced lines to 64 characters (they are not wrapped in the reader's
  512-unit column), and tables to a few short columns.
